b'I'm sorry if this it the wrong place to report this, but I haven't found a better one so far.

I've started playing with your library and found custom_query interface very difficult to work with. There are two main reasons behind it.

### Docs examples are confusing/broken
I've tried implementing the highlighting from https://docs.haystack.deepset.ai/reference/retriever-api#bm25retriever__init__, but it's not clear how it works. The example shows invalid python snippet.

After reading the code, I've figured it's actually the string that should be passed. I suggest that docs are changed to something like
```py
custom_query = """
    "size": 10,
    "query": {
        "bool": {
            "should": [
                {
                    "multi_match": {
                        "query": ${query},
                        "type": "most_fields",
                        "fields": ["content", "title"]
                    }
                }
            ]
        }
    },
    "highlight": {
        "fields": {
            "content": {},
            "title": {}
        }
    }
}
"""
```

### Passing a string in given format is very difficult to iterate on
ES queries are valid JSON structures, which map really nicely to python dicts. That's how most ES queries would be created - stats with simple dictionary, maybe some conditional clauses are added later on, depending on the shape of the query or selected filters.

The interface of most ES clients is to accept either a dict or instance of some Query class. 
Accepting a string is fine too, if it's easy to convert the dictionary query to the query template (e.g. `json.dumps(query)`). 

Unfortunately, it's not possible with haystack because library replaces `${query}` with `"_query_content_"` instead of `_query_content_` (wraps with quotes).

If you look at the query above, part of it is
```
  "multi_match": { "query": ${query},
```

and not 
```
  "multi_match": { "query": "${query}",
```

which prevents you from passing custom query like
```py
bm25_retriever = BM25Retriever(
    document_store=document_store,
    custom_query=json.dumps(custom_query),
)
```

Replacing `${query}` with non-wrapped version would avoid that issue at least partially.

----


As the current workaround, I work with the query like dicts, and massage the custom query template just before passing it

```py
# query as dict
query_with_highligting = {
    "size": 10,
    "query": {
        "bool": {
            "should": [
                {
                    "multi_match": {
                        "query": "${query}",
                        "type": "most_fields",
                        "fields": ["content", "title"]
                    }
                }
            ]
        }
    },
    "highlight": {
        "fields": {
            "content": {
                "pre_tags" : ["<u>"],
                "post_tags" : ["</u>"]
            },
            "title": {
                "pre_tags" : ["<u>"],
                "post_tags" : ["</u>"]
            }
        }
    }
}


document_store = filled_document_store(docs)
bm25_retriever = BM25Retriever(
    document_store=document_store,
    # make sure query is not quoted
    custom_query=json.dumps(query_with_highligting).replace('"${query}"', '${query}'),
)

```

It works, but it took me a while to figure it out. I suspect others will bump into similar trouble.'

b'@ZanSara Pin should be updated to `>=0.3.1` as pyworld introduced support for Python 3.10.

The current pyworld pin was introduced to allow Haystack installation, as there as a dependency conflict between pyworld 0.3 and other modules (numpy related).

@nickchomey This is the reason you can now use, 0.3.1 update.'

b'Thank you @julian-risch for the detailed explanation!

@bogdankostic yes, you are right. It is failing at `json.loads` because of `${query}` placeholder.

As a workaround, since `${query}` placeholder is mandatory I have commented out `JSON` validation check for `custom_query` and it is working as expected. I will see what else can be done for permanent fix and will post it here.'

b"@youxingtian Thank you for reporting this bug. The error message indicates that `document_count = len(result)` can't be executed because result is of type 'generator', which has no `len()`.
To fix this bug, we would need to replace the line with something like

`document_count = self.get_document_count(index=index)`. However, `get_document_count` is not implemented yet for `Milvus1DocumentStore`. It has been implemented only for Milvus2DocumentStore so far here: https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/blob/75641dd024976591b96285e6eb7cded6254f87d5/haystack/document_stores/sql.py#L524

@masci I am surprised that this bug didn't show up in our tests here: https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/blob/75641dd024976591b96285e6eb7cded6254f87d5/test/document_stores/test_sql_based.py#L54

@youxingtian May I ask what version of Haystack you are using? Is it the latest version of the main branch or one of the releases?"

b"Hi @giguru, this is a great suggestion. I am currently working on evaluation nodes at #904  and my focus is on open domain QA (ODQA). The idea is to build a pipeline like this:
Query --> `Retriever` --> `EvalRetriever` --> `Reader` --> `EvalReader`

`EvalRetriever` would be the place to put the IR style metrics. It has as input the documents that have been retrieved as well as the label for a single query. One change that would have be made is that the format of `label` will need to change since for ODQA it is simply just an answer string. But I could imagine label being a dictionary of different labels

```python
label = {
    'reader': 'text answer to the question',
    'retriever': {
        'doc_2': 1,
        'doc_3': 1,
    }
}
``` 
Does this sound good to you? Are there any other requirements I might be missing here?"

b"ok, thanks for the clarification! the diff on CPU might be related to multiprocessing.

@ZanSara @vblagoje can one of you please take over here and try to replicate this? If the gap is consistently that huge on CPU it might make sense to open a new issue about it.

@vblagoje Weren't you investigating to get rid of multiprocessing anyway?"

b'Hello,
Quick update. I managed to deploy a simple Retriever-Reader-Pipeline fastapi application with docker-compose to AWS. I have used elasticsearch and selenium images and made some changes in Crawler. It consumes around 3gb memory.

Given urls and questions list, it returns question and answers.

If this is the way to go for 'Simpler deployment', I can work on PR to convert my docker-compose.yaml to helm chart, and make necessary changes.


Example: 

`{
    "url_list": ["https://www.adaptiv-networks.com/post/qsrs-and-fast-casual-restaurants-are-improving-operations-with-adaptiv-sd-wan"],
    "questions_list": ["What is the challenge?"]
}`

`{
        "Question": "What is the challenge?",
        "Answers": {
                "answer": "restaurant teams struggle to succeed when their network connectivity is not delivering",
                "score": 0.22019997239112854,
                "context": "onnectivity for staff training, restaurant teams struggle to succeed when their network connectivity is not delivering. Adaptiv Eliminates the Challen",
                "document_id": "34ab9a8ae484e4bfe5c27ef6c814bef6",
                "meta": {
                    "url": "https://www.adaptiv-networks.com/post/qsrs-and-fast-casual-restaurants-are-improving-operations-with-adaptiv-sd-wan",
                    "_split_id": 1
                }
          }
}`'

b"Another option for `doc.meta['classification']` could be like the following:
```
{
  'label': 'music',
  'details': {
                 'music': 0.953018069267273,
                 'history': 0.023982945829629898,
                 'natural language processing': 0.02299901656806469
                 }
}
```

The `label` field may allow straight-forward filtering/routing,
while `details` would contain complete information on the classification."

b"Hi @kalki7, 

Ah yes, I think you have it correct that the `embedding_dim` set during `FAISSDocumentStore` initialization must match the dimension produced by the retriever model which will update the embeddings later. So one must know it during initialization.

@bogdankostic Is the above understanding correct? I guess it means we can't arbitrarily use any model in the retriever once the store is initialized which is probably not the end of the world but maybe inconvenient. Or is there an approach to make this a non-issue? "

b"They seem to use multiple prediction heads which we have already implemented in `AdaptiveModel`. However they use an additional feedforward network to determine which prediction head should be used to generate the answer.

@lalitpagaria have you analyzed this a bit more, what would need to be done here?
@julian-risch can you take a look at this. Maybe it's easier than I think..."

b'**Describe the bug**
Trying to load an OpensearchDocumentStore with a JSON string as value for the `custom_mapping` field fails.

**Error message**
Validation fails as the string contains forbidden chars, like `*`

**Expected behavior**
This field should accept a string containing JSON data, and should validate that the string contains valid JSON.

**Additional context**
Reported by @ArzelaAscoIi 

**To Reproduce**
```python
import json
from haystack import Pipeline

configuration = {
    "version": "ignore",
    "components": [{
        "name": "DocumentStore",
        "type": "OpenSearchDocumentStore",
        "params": {
            "port": 9200,
            "scheme": "https",
            "verify_certs": False,
            "create_index": True,
            "content_field": "content",
            "search_fields": "content",
            "embedding_dim": 768,
            "embedding_field": "embedding",
            "excluded_meta_data": False,
            "custom_mapping": json.dumps({
                "mappings": {
                    "properties": {
                        "content": {
                            "type": "text"
                        },
                        "name": {
                            "type": "keyword"
                        }
                    }
                },
                "settings": {
                    "index": {
                        "knn": "true"
                    }
                },
            }),
            "host": "localhost",
            "username": "admin",
            "password": "admin"
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "Retriever",
        "type": "ElasticsearchRetriever",
        "params": {
            "document_store": "DocumentStore",
            "top_k": 5
        }
    }],
    "pipelines": [{
        "name": "query",
        "nodes": [{
            "name": "Retriever",
            "inputs": [
                "Query"
            ]
        }]
    }]
}

p = Pipeline.load_from_config(configuration, pipeline_name="query")
```

**FAQ Check**
- [X] Have you had a look at [our new FAQ page](https://haystack.deepset.ai/overview/faq)?
'

b'We recently added a `run_batch()` method in every pipeline node, which can increase inference speed when batching multiple queries: https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/pull/2481
However, `pipeline.eval()` internally still calls `pipeline.run()` and evaluation is slow. We should change that so that `pipeline.eval()` calls `pipeline.run_batch()` and thereby increase evaluation speed.

@Timoeller brought up this issue'

b'Hi all. My use-case is that I'm trying to filter documents on ES that have a list field (or in fact, a keyword field with multiple values) and I want to hit only the documents that have the same combination of values in that field as the list provided. 

For example, let's say there are 4 documents:

```
doc1 = {"myfield": [x, y , z]}
doc2 = {"myfield": [x, y]}
doc3 = {"myfield": [x, z]}
doc4 = {"myfield": [x]}

filter = {"myfield": {"$eq": [x, y]}}
```

In this case the query should only match doc2. Doc1 and doc4 should not be matched because they have more values, and less values, respectively. An doc3 has different values.

It seems that the "$eq" operator with a list as value is a special case that is not supported in the ElasticsearchDocumentStore. 
The "$in" operator is not suitable either.
I don't fully understand how Elasticsearch fields work but the documentation says that any field can have any number of values, like a list, but it seems that Haystack cannot query fields with more than one value.

**I wonder if it is possible to achieve this effect by using the current haystack implementation. If so, I'd appreciate any help.**

One solution that I've found in the ES documentation is the terms set using the minimum_should_match_script as shown here:
https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/elasticsearch/reference/7.10/query-dsl-terms-set-query.html#terms-set-query-script

I've tried replacing the assertion inside document_stores.filter_utils.EqOperation.convert_to_elasticsearch() (line 421) with this code:
```
if isinstance(self.comparison_value, list):
            return {
                "terms_set": {
                    self.field_name: {
                        "terms": self.comparison_value,
                        "minimum_should_match_script": {
                            "source": f"Math.max(params.num_terms, doc['{self.field_name}'].size())"
            }}}}
```
but I must be doing something wrong because it doesn't work... 

Thanks in advance! :)'

