b'Hey @SAIVENKATARAJU,

you're right. This is a bug. The exception however is intended to spot such field type issues:
what's happening here is, that you're creating the index the first time you create the `ElasticsearchDocumentStore`. As it is a new one, no field types are checked: no error. However the index creation has a bug. All `self.search_fields` should be created with type `text`. However they are created with type `keyword`. And that's what the check the next time you instantiate `ElasticsearchDocumentStore` complains about. If the check wasn't there, the search would work in general, but you would not actually search in the fields specified by self.search_fields but "content".

So to cleanly fix it we have to set all self.search_fields to type `text` such as in https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/blob/c6f23dce8897ab00fcb15e272282d459dcfa564a/haystack/document_stores/elasticsearch.py#L278

Quick workaround:
Pass the following custom_mapping to `ElasticsearchDocumentStore`'s constructor:
```python
mapping = {
                "mappings": {
                    "properties": {
                        "name": {"type": "text"},
                        "content": {"type": "text"},
                        "page_title": {"type": "text"},
                    },
                    "dynamic_templates": [
                        {
                            "strings": {
                                "path_match": "*",
                                "match_mapping_type": "string",
                                "mapping": {"type": "keyword"}}}
                    ],
                },
                "settings": {
                    "analysis": {
                        "analyzer": {
                            "default": {
                                "type": "standard",
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
```

@SAIVENKATARAJU, if you like to fix the `ElasticsearchDocumentStore` implementation in a PR, please give me short sign. Otherwise I will do it... This should be fixed quickly I guess.'

b'@SjSnowball I would suggest to first support the `custom_query` way only, which is just about propagading the highlight results from the elasticsearch response to our meta field. This will be needed for the "usual" query anyway. So you can start with this. Btw, I would prefer to not extend the `_source` object here, but add the highlighting results explicitly to `meta_data`.

For the usual query (without `custom_query`) we would have to extend the constructor for at least three additional highlighting params to support the same flexibility as with `custom_query`. 
@tholor what do you think about it? Do we want to support highlighting for usual queries, too? Should we make it as configurable as with `custom_query` like setting custom highlighting tags or shouldn't we rather rely on the default highlighting tags of the index (default in elasticsearch is `<em>` and `</em>`) as it is THE default way?'

